abstract={ABSTRACTUrban noise can disrupt avian communication networks, resulting in reduced communication among individuals. Mountain chickadees alter their vocalisations in areas of high ambient noise; however, it is unknown how audible their signals are within the nest cavity, where the female remains during dawn signalling. To test the relative audibility of songs within the nest box of urban and rural chickadees, we conducted a playback experiment directed at empty nest boxes using an autonomous recording unit above the box with external microphones and an internal microphone inside the nest box. We placed a speaker 25-75 m from the nest box and played two playback files of mountain chickadee songs – one with added noise and one without. Songs had a higher signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) in rural habitats and in ‘Without Added Noise’ treatments, however, SNR decreased with increasing distance from the nest box. The lower frequency song notes had a lower SNR inside the nest in both treatment types. As expected, noise and distance resulted in decreased SNR of chickadee songs within the nest cavity. These findings suggest a possible constraint in urban birds requiring them to reduce their distance from the nest to ensure audibility to the female while in noise.KEYWORDS: Birdsongfemale perceptionmountain chickadeenest boxsignal-to-noise ratiourbanisation AcknowledgementsWe wish to acknowledge our work was conducted on the unceded territories of the Tk’emlúps te Secwépemc and Skeetchestn First Nations, and Lheidli T’enneh, part of the Dakelh (Carrier), First Nations. We thank field assistants B. Smith, M. Oud, A. Lamberton, and numerous volunteers provided by Thompson Rivers University, as well as K. Legeard for assisting with initial data preparations and Dr. S. LaZerte for assisting with preliminary data organisation and coding script preparations. R. Dawson and J. Foote kindly provided feedback on previous drafts of this manuscript. We also acknowledge the City of Kamloops for permitting us to conduct our studies in parks and urban areas.Disclosure statementAll authors declare that they have no established conflicting financial interests or personal relationships that may have influenced the research presented in this paper.Data availability statementData can be requested from the Corresponding Author.Ethical statementOur study followed the institutional and national ethical guidelines for scientific research in Canada, the home country of our institutions. All work was approved by the University of Northern British Columbia Animal Care and Use Committee (UNBC-2017-01 and UNBC-2020-01) and was conducted under a Canadian Federal Master Banding Permit and Scientific Collection Permit no. 10940.Additional informationFundingThis work was supported by Project Learning Tree Canada under Project ID [80482]; Natural Sciences of Engineering Research Council of Canada Discovery Grant under grant [RGPIN-2019-07045 and 4603-2018]; the University of Northern British Columbia through a British Columbia Graduate Scholarship; the University of Northern British Columbia Research Project Award under grant [29232] and an Indspire Almina Pokrant Award.},
language={},
doi={10.1080/09524622.2023.2172080},
}

@ARTICLE{315,
