abstract={Department of Ecology, Evolution, and Organismal Biology, Iowa State University, Ames, Iowa 50011ABSTRACT Temperature affects growth and develop-ment, and morphometry can provide a quantitativedescription of how temperature changes affect the result-ing phenotype. We performed a morphometric analysis onzebra ﬁnch (Taeniopygia guttata) embryos that were ei-ther exposed to periodic cooling to 20 or 308C throughoutincubation over a background temperature of 37.58C, orwere incubated at a constant temperature of 37.58C.Using a principle components analysis, we found that therelationship between the multivariate size (ﬁrst principlecomponent) and dry embryo mass depended upon thethermal treatment to which the developing embryos wereexposed. Periodic cooling resulted in a smaller embryomass, but had no effect on the multivariate size of theembryo. This suggests that the growth of phenotypictraits such as the length of long bones and the skull areless affected by temperature than is growth of other softtissues such as muscle and organs that contribute to bodymass. J. Morphol. 269:875–883, 2008.},
