abstract={3, with an initial density of 2.5 ind/m 3 . The successful year we used pup food and larvae of Diptera, together with seed mixture and rearing food, to feed the Sparrows. The use of mealworms during the 1999 breeding season was fruitless because they have toxins that are poisonous for nestlings and when used in abundance provoke their death. In the year 2000, there was an increase in almost all reproductive parameters like number of clutches, clutch size or percentage of clutches where at least one egg hatched. 38 nestlings left the nest in 2000 versus 0 in 1999. Reproductive parameters in 2000 (such as clutch size or incubation period) were similar to those found in natural conditions. The breeding season was longer and the number of clutches per nest was higher in captivity than in natural conditions. Other parameters (especially fledging and breeding success) were lower, and at the bottom limit of those values found in natural conditions. We also detected cases of intraspecific brood parasitism and infanticide that are discussed.},
